  area  placing 366 of them in foster or  adoptive homes all amid the corona virus  pandemic the animal welfare community is  really struggling with the balance  between saving the animals lives and  saving the people's lives how much  personal interaction is needed to save a  life they're all life or death  transports if we do not take these  animals from the shelter's they are  going to be killed and we don't want  that result particularly when we know so  many people will benefit emotionally  spiritually even physically right like  having a dog makes you go out and go for  a walk in our new normal where Times  Square here is virtually empty I can  tell you that having an animal like my  guy Berkeley really is good for the soul  some of the animal welfare people that I  spoke with said right now it feels like  these guys are the only ones who are  winning but one of their concerns is  what happens to our four-legged friends  a few months from now so there's so much  uncertainty and there's so many unknowns  Matt Berger is president and CEO of the  ASPCA the economic reality of this  healthcare crisis and the economic  crisis are very very real and it's  absolutely concerned about increased  surrenders and abandonment to help the  ASPCA created a five million dollar  rescue and relief fund with two goals  keeping shelter's afloat if donations  dry up and assisting struggling pet  owners with food distribution more  people will need help caring for their  pets and keeping those pets in the home  that's the idea behind providing these  supplies not only to keep that bond in  place in times of crisis when people  rely on their animals for psychological  support but to keep those animals out of  the shelter good job keeping families  together like Carly Tom and Milly  who in a little more than a week formed  what the animal rescue world calls a  forever home I know a lot of people in  the rescue world will say when we go out  to rescue a dog you pick your dog you  save them but the other side of it is  the dog saves you in some way do you  guys feel bad already  I definitely  Carly wakes up everyday and says I don't  know what I would do without this dog  yeah the whole idea of having a purpose  and something to focus your energy on  during this time has been really  wonderful it's so nice to see how much  she loves him she is definitely a  daddy's girl she loves him so much so  it's an awesome and obviously these  rescues are still going on I know people  here that shelters don't have pets there  are still pets out there there are a lot  of transports from the South they're  being very careful in trying to socially  distance when they do them and I think  the most important word Jeff is that if  you are struggling to keep your pet  reach out to some of these organizations  that can help you with food and other  resources someone hopefully will be  there for you Dana I want to  congratulate Berkeley on staying still  30 seconds there during that during your  stand if that was very impressive you  look like a like a step beautiful statue  you know and Dan there was a bit of a  Megan Marco look-alike in your piece I  really enjoyed it thank you

